<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sudan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sudan refers both to the modern country on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and, historically, to the bilad al-Sudan, the broad belt of West and Central African <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">savannah</a> south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. Arab geographers used the term for the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu</a> empires. The term appears in many profiles, including those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sudan.jpg" alt="Sudan" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sudan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The modern state of Sudan covers 1,886,068 km2, the third-largest country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> by area, with an 853 km coast on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>. The terrain is generally flat plains broken by mountain ranges, and the highest point is the Deriba Caldera, at 3,042 metres, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrah-mountains">Marrah Mountains</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Blue Nile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a> meet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a> to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, which flows north through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><p>The north and centre are extremely dry, with the Nubian and Bayuda deserts, while the south has grasslands and tropical savanna. The name comes from the Arabic bilad as-sudan, the Land of the Blacks, which was historically applied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> to Northeast Africa; before that the area was known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oldest evidence is the camp remains at Affad 23, some 50,000 years old, and the war of Jebel Sahaba, around 11500 BCE, is described as the earliest known war in the world. The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> flourished from about 2500 to 1500 BCE and in its last phase became a populous empire rivalling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Egyptian rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> then held the region, beginning when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-i">Thutmose I</a> occupied Kush and destroyed Kerma. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a> lasted from about 785 BCE to 350 CE, and after its fall the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubians">Nubians</a> formed the Christian kingdoms of Nobatia, Makuria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alodia">Alodia</a>.</p><p>Arab nomads settled most of Sudan between the 14th and 15th centuries. From the 16th to the 19th centuries the Funj sultanate dominated the centre and east, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> ruled the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> the east. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> conquered the whole country in the 19th century. After the Mahdist Uprising was defeated by a joint Egyptian-British force, Egypt agreed in 1899 under British pressure to share <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> with the United Kingdom, and Sudan was in effect governed as a British possession. It became independent on 1 January 1956.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sudan has known repeated civil war. The first, from 1955 to 1972, was followed by a second between government forces and southern rebels, which led to the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a> in 2011. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a> law applied from 1983 until 2020, when Sudan became a secular state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sudan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ezana's inscriptions, including the trilingual Ezana Stone in Ge'ez, Sabaean and Greek, record campaigns against peoples across what is now <b>Sudan</b>, Eritrea and the Ethiopian interior, and present the king as a divinely sanctioned conqueror who destroyed towns and deported populations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 14th and 15th centuries Arab tribes overran much of <b>Sudan</b>, perhaps aided by a plague in the mid-14th century; the collapse of Makuria late in the century, in Adams's phrase, burst the flood gates open.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Al-Zubayr's defeat and killing of Sultan Ibrahim in 1874 ended the first sultanate, and Darfur was annexed to Egyptian <b>Sudan</b>; in 1883–1884 it fell to the forces of the Mahdi, and Keira authority remained in abeyance until 1898.</blockquote><a href="/empire/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Kutama Berbers formed the original revolutionary core; Turkish slave soldiers, the ghilman, were recruited for their skill as mounted archers; Black African infantry from the <b>Sudan</b> formed a large and politically assertive contingent.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A decree of 16 June 1895 placed Senegal, French <b>Sudan</b>, French Guinea and Ivory Coast under a governor-general, who was also governor of Senegal at Saint-Louis, to coordinate military and economic policy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Many families of holy men whose authority dates from this period remained socially and politically important in the <b>Sudan</b> into the twentieth century, and the Qadiriyya and other orders introduced under the Funj became central to Sudanese religious life.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funj-sultanate">Funj Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
